today we're going to look at Mendeleev a  Russian chemist from the 19th century in  1869 he first presented his periodic  table which is shown on the screen here  to a big audience of scientists and many  of them actually laughed him off the  stage and he was very disturbed by this  however his periodic table is largely  unchanged for the one that we see in  today's chemistry books and he was a  true genius to put his periodic table  together  what Mendeleev did was he decided to  organize his periodic table of elements  and at the time there were only 50  elements known he organized his periodic  table by looking at their mass so you  can see at the start he had hydrogen  then lithium beryllium boron carbon  nitrogen oxygen fluorine going across  but also when he started to arrange them  he noticed patterns starting to occur so  he noticed that when he arranged them in  this particular sequence that the  elements in each column had similar  chemical properties so for instance he  noticed that in the first column all of  the elements reacted with water to  produce oxides so not only did his  periodic table show the order of  elements by mass but also we showed that  they have similar chemical properties in  each particular column other things that  came out from his periodic table was  that sometimes he noticed that elements  were actually in the wrong order and he  switched them round this is a particular  stroke of genius because there are some  elements whose masses don't correspond  to their atomic number and this is  because they may have slightly more  neutrons they may have different  isotopes and the result is that overall  when you measure them they give a  heavier mass than you'd expect he  overlooked this and I've just popped  them in based on their properties so his  periodic table was arranged by mass  and that's important to realize that's  mass not mass number but he arranged  them by their mass and he linked that  into their properties another stroke of  genius by Mendeleev was to realize that  although he had 50 elements that there  may well be some elements not discovered  in other words some missing elements and  as a result he had the sense to leave  some spaces on his periodic table these  spaces indicated here as the asterixis  however because he knew that these  elements had similar properties to the  ones that were around them in the same  column and also he noticed that there  were already patterns developing in  terms of their physical properties he  was able to predict particular  properties now in specific this element  here which we now know to be germanium  Mendeleev he called it eka silicon and  he predicted that it had particular  properties he predicted its density its  mass and how it would react and when  germanium was in finally discovered they  didn't find in fact find that it had  very similar properties so those  predicted by Mendeleev now there are  still some differences between our  modern periodic table and our modern  periodic table is actually organized by  atomic number not mass or mass number  and in addition there is a whole group  of elements that are not mentioned on  Mendeleev's periodic table at all and  those are the noble gases or group 8 and  that's because these gases were  discovered very late on because of  course although their gases and are in  the air around us they do not react  and so therefore you don't find the many  Ning compounds and they were unaware  that they existed until into the 20th  century so there is a whole group eight  that does not appear on the end of  Mendeleev's periodic table so in summary  Mendeleev was not the first person but  the first person to put together a  periodic table that is very similar to  the one that we use today in chemistry  he organized it by mass and identified  patterns within the properties of the  elements that he put into his particular  periodic table because of this he was  able to identify gaps in his periodic  table and predict the properties of the  elements that were delayed to be  discovered to fill these gaps the modern  periodic table differs from Mendeleev in  that it is ordered by the atomic number  and has the noble gases which were not  present at all in Mendeleev's periodic  table
 

